## Two deaths, one month apart

Frontrunners Athletes recently shared a post that stopped us in our tracks. Two professional rugby players, Luqobo Makwedini and Saimoni Vunilagi, died after training in extreme heat, on two different continents, one month apart. Both had their best years ahead of them.

You can read the original post here: [Frontrunners Athletes on Instagram](https://www.instagram.com/p/Db2PFI8mTqN/)

These were elite athletes with full-time medical and conditioning support around them. If heat can take a life at that level, it is worth asking hard questions about what protection exists for junior and grassroots athletes, who usually have none of that support.

## Why this matters for community sport

Most junior sport in Australia is run by volunteer coaches and committees, often without a hydration policy, without a heat guideline that gets followed on the day, and without anyone whose job it is to call off training when conditions turn dangerous.

Heat stress does not announce itself. Kids keep playing because they do not want to let the team down, and coaches keep training running because the schedule says so. That combination is exactly how these situations turn serious.

## What grassroots clubs can do now

A few practical steps that cost nothing and take five minutes to put in place:

- Check the Bureau of Meteorology heat and UV forecast before training, not just on match day
- Set a plain rule for your club: above a certain temperature, training is shortened or moved to shade, no exceptions and no discretion left to whoever happens to be running the session
- Build in a water break every fifteen to twenty minutes as standard, not as something a player has to ask for
- Watch for the early signs, dizziness, confusion, stopping sweating, and know that any one of them means training stops immediately
- Talk to your association about whether a heat policy already exists for your sport, and if it does, make sure everyone coaching actually knows what it says

## Our part in this

Tiny Sports exists to help grassroots clubs and coaches access the funding and support that bigger, better resourced organisations take for granted. Equipment grants can extend to shade structures, cooling gear, and hydration stations, not just uniforms and balls. If your club is thinking about what it would take to train safely through an Australian summer, get in touch and we will help you work out what is fundable.

Rest in peace, Luqobo and Saimoni.

